<h2>The statutory map</h2>
<p>The DV Act separates its reliefs by function. Section 18 protects (restraint orders); Section 19 houses (residence orders); Section 20 funds (monetary relief for expenses incurred and losses suffered); Section 21 arranges custody; Section 22 compensates (damages for injuries, including mental torture and emotional distress). Section 23 then supplies the temporal dimension: subsection (1) is the general interim power, and subsection (2) permits even ex parte orders, on affidavit, across the named sections — Section 22 included.</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Section 20: restitutionary.</strong> It reimburses and sustains — loss of earnings, medical expenses, loss caused by destruction or removal of property, and maintenance. It is designed to operate on incomplete information, &ldquo;adequate, fair and reasonable&rdquo; on the material available.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Section 22: compensatory.</strong> It awards damages for injury caused by acts of domestic violence &ldquo;committed by that respondent&rdquo; — a formulation that looks backward to proven conduct and sounds in adjudication.</div>
</div>

<h2>Reading Section 23(2) honestly</h2>
<p>An honest reading accepts both halves of the picture. Parliament did include Section 22 in the ex parte interim provision, and a Magistrate therefore has the <em>power</em> to pass an interim order referable to it; a submission that interim compensation is wholly incompetent overstates the position. But power and propriety are different things. Compensation for mental torture and emotional distress cannot be meaningfully quantified on affidavits at the threshold of a contested case: the extent of injury, causation, and the respondent&rsquo;s role are precisely what the trial will determine. An interim award of damages risks prejudging the central issue — whether domestic violence occurred at all — and creates recovery complications if the application ultimately fails.</p>

<h2>The practical middle path</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Immediate needs → Section 20 interim.</strong> Medical bills, alternative accommodation rent under Section 19(1)(f), subsistence — claimed with documents and an income affidavit.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Quantifiable past losses → Section 20 again.</strong> Destroyed or withheld property, stridhan detention, lost earnings have monetary measures a Magistrate can assess prima facie.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Injury and distress → Section 22 at final hearing.</strong> Plead it from the outset, support it with medical and psychological evidence as the case proceeds, and press it when findings are available.</div>
</div>

<h2>For respondents</h2>
<p>A respondent facing an interim compensation prayer should resist it on structure rather than sympathy: point out that the claim, as framed, requires findings the court cannot yet record; that the applicant&rsquo;s immediate needs are fully addressable under Section 20; and that any interim figure for distress would be arbitrary by definition. Simultaneously, contest the Section 20 arithmetic on evidence — income affidavits cut both ways, and inflated interim claims deflate quickly when tested against documents. If an interim compensation order is nonetheless passed, Section 29 provides the appellate route to the Court of Session within thirty days.</p>

<h2>Drafting pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Applicants: separate the heads of relief in the interim application — one consolidated &ldquo;lump sum&rdquo; prayer invites the objection that compensation is being smuggled in at the interim stage.</p>
  <p>Keep a contemporaneous loss file: prescriptions, bills, photographs, police complaints. Section 20 interim relief is won on paper.</p>
  <p>Respondents: file the income affidavit honestly and early; credibility on money matters colours the Magistrate&rsquo;s view of everything else.</p>
  <p>Both sides: remember that interim orders are alterable under Section 25(2) on changed circumstances — neither victory nor defeat at this stage is permanent.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer discusses the statutory scheme in general terms for information only. It is not legal advice, and the approach of individual courts varies with the facts.</div>
